                  "system": "http://loinc.org",
                  "display": "Summarization of encounter note"
                }
              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the hospital course of your 75-year-old female patient, admitted on 24/10/1987 with acute lower abdominal pain and suspected tubal pregnancy. On admission, physical examination revealed peritoneal signs. Transvaginal ultrasonography confirmed a tubal ectopic pregnancy with signs of early rupture. Serum beta-hCG was markedly elevated. Given her clinical deterioration and age-related risk for rupture, the patient underwent laparoscopic salpingectomy with successful removal of the ectopic pregnancy. Intraoperative findings confirmed a ruptured right tubal pregnancy. Postoperative recovery was uneventful. Intravenous fluid replacement and analgesic medication were administered perioperatively. Vital signs remained stable throughout the stay. The patient was discharged on 27/10/1987 in good general condition. Please ensure follow-up including monitoring of beta-hCG levels to confirm complete resolution. Kind regards.</div>"
            },
            "code": {
